A||||27||billfredmike24||Cars for dummies||||1||
Z||000000||billfredmike24||12-12-1999||08:00 PM||||What would you guys suggest for a guy that knows how to turn the ignition key, and thats about it, on how to learn about cars.  Books, reading on the internet, anything? Please help, I guess to be a fiero owner I'm gonna need to know how to do my own repairs.  <BR>Thanks for your help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||12-12-1999||08:14 PM||||I think a Chilton Fiero manual and a book about general auto-repair would be a good place to start. Next step would be a Haynes manual and perhaps the workshop manual.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-12-1999||11:26 PM||||Plus, if you have cable, TNN all day long on sundays. I learn a ton of stuff from all those shows. Too bad they never work on Fieros though.||||reg||1
Z||000003||dogtired||12-13-1999||11:18 AM||||Bill, Fred, and Mike 24<BR>Does that mean there are 24 of you three guys?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Anyway stick with us here, This forum covers everything from moth-balls to total rebuild. If you will note in past threads/posts we also cover many subjects many times. From: "How do I set my clock" to<BR>"Clutch revisited"<BR>Sometimes we make fun of questions but we usually always answer them, in some manner. And most of us don't know where you live so we can't come after you for asking silly questions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>So ask here & I will try and make fun of you, er we will try and answer your questions.<BR>This is one of the GREATEST lists, and a FUN place to learn!  Manuals do help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Father-of-Fiero-owner||12-13-1999||09:40 PM||||They are right billfredmike24 especially Dogtired. This is the GREATEST Fiero site that I've found. I got 5 replies to my question about my kids car in less than 24 hrs. So ask the questions and I'm sure someone will make fun of you errrr I mean help you out. REALLY GOOD INFO on this site.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||12-13-1999||10:01 PM||||bilfredmike- your name reminds me of the scam I do to telemarketers. My published phone number uses my middle name- teacher's trick to keep from being harassed at home by angry parents- and I have 2 additional unpublished numbers. Whenever someone calls and asks to speak to Alan, I know that they don't know me, so I tell them that Alan's personality is currently out of body, but an alternate ego has been selected to replace him on a temporary basis. They may speak, but no one at this residence is mentally competent to make purchasing decisions. I hardly ever have a telemarketer call back. I can't figure out why!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||12-14-1999||04:27 AM||||LOL!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I know this is completely off-topic, but when a telemarketer calls you might also wanna try saying: "<I>You're one of <B>them</B>, aren't you? Oh my gawd, you're one of them!</I>" (Scream and hang up). They won't call you again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||11:48 AM||||Good one, Cliff!  And when someone persistent comes to the door (I won't mention any groups out of fear that someone here is a member), try answering the door naked.  They won't come back!  (Hey...we were already off-topic)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Sage||12-14-1999||04:04 PM||||while we're off topic:  a friend of mine has a pat response to telemarketers: no matter what they are pushing, he tells them," oh yea, I just ordered that yesterday!"  they usually don't call back.  As to your original question, you could do what I did when I was 16...get a car that you don't need to use and take it apart, one piece at a time, then put it back together!  Actually looking at the different systems and seeing how they are put together and how they work, does wonders for your accumulated knowledge.  There are many good books, but I still think "hands on" teaches the best. What's that?...not practical!  Oh well I guess you can read about it!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||12-14-1999||04:50 PM||||<BR>I believe that the best combination is to use books AND "hands-on". It never ceases to amaze me when someone posts a question here about something real basic. Something that would be easy to find out about if they just went to the library and did some simple research. It's only my opinion, but I think the forum is better served when people save their questions for something that has them stumped, not something they haven't even tried to investigate yet on their own. I enjoy helping people out here, but not when I can tell they've made NO effort themselves. <P>Maybe I'm just old-fashioned (or grumpy today).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000010||Sage||12-14-1999||06:49 PM||||Patrick:  Maybe more self reliant than most, and everybody has an "off" day now and then.  I thnk this is just a question from someone who wanted to see if there were any "secrets" "magic" or "tricks" <B>before</B> they struck out on their own?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Patrick||12-14-1999||07:14 PM||||<BR>Sage, please don't misunderstand me. I wasn't in any way being critical of the 24guys <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> who started this thread. I think the questions posed were definitely worth asking. <P>However, there are... ahem... a couple of others at this forum who seem to ask an awful lot of real basic mechanical questions. But hey, as long as there's people here happy to answer them, fine.<P>I've found that when I've had a problem and sat down to study a manual, that I've usually learned about a whole lot more than I had originally planned. That to me is the beauty of doing one's own research and investigation.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||14
